# Annual Report -> seed_financials.csv extractor

Populates `data/seed_financials.csv` from audited annual report PDFs
(NGX filings). One CSV row = one ticker + one financial year.

## Why this is two steps, not one "auto-fill" command

Nigerian issuers format their financial statements very differently
(banks vs. non-banks, different comparative-year layouts, figures stated
in `N'000` vs `N'million` vs full Naira, etc). A script that silently
writes numbers straight into the master CSV risks putting a wrong figure
into a real financial dataset with no way to notice. So the pipeline is:

1. `extract.py` reads a PDF and writes a **candidates JSON** file with
   every value it found, the page/table it came from, and the raw text
   it matched — nothing touches the CSV yet.
2. You open the JSON, compare each value against the PDF for that ticker
   and year, and fix anything wrong or missing.
3. `merge_into_csv.py` writes the reviewed JSON into the CSV. It only
   fills cells that are currently blank — it will never overwrite a
   value that's already there — and formats every monetary figure as a
   full Naira integer (no commas, no ₦, no abbreviations), leaving
   not-applicable fields blank.

## Setup

```bash
cd pdf_financial_extractor
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## 1. Extract candidates from a PDF

```bash
python extract.py \
  --pdf "/path/to/GTCO_FY2025_Annual_Report.pdf" \
  --ticker GTCO \
  --period-end 2025-12-31 \
  --source-doc "FY2025 Audited Results - NGX filing" \
  --out candidates_GTCO_2025.json
```

This:
- Extracts every table and text line from the PDF with `pdfplumber`.
- Matches row/line labels against the line-item patterns in `config.py`
  (revenue, gross earnings, profit before tax, total assets, deposits
  from customers, etc — see `LABEL_PATTERNS`).
- Detects the unit the statement is expressed in (`N'000`, `N million`,
  "in thousands of Naira", ...) from nearby page text and multiplies
  values up to full Naira amounts.
- Handles `(123,456)`-style negatives (impairment charges, tax expense,
  cost of sales are usually shown this way).
- Skips columns that don't apply to the entity type (banks don't have
  `cost_of_sales`/`gross_profit`/`operating_profit`/`ebitda`; non-banks
  don't have `net_interest_income`/`impairment_charge`/
  `customer_deposits`/`loans_advances` — see `ENTITY_TYPE` in
  `config.py`, add new tickers there as needed).

Open the resulting JSON and check `fields.<column>.value` against the
PDF page listed in `candidates`. Edit the `value` directly for anything
missing or wrong.

## 2. Merge reviewed candidates into the CSV

```bash
python merge_into_csv.py \
  --csv ../data/seed_financials.csv \
  --candidates candidates_GTCO_2025.json
```

Only blank cells for the matching `(ticker, period_end)` row are filled;
everything else in the CSV is left exactly as-is. Pass
`--allow-overwrite` only if you deliberately want to replace an existing
value.

Repeat steps 1–2 once per (ticker, financial year) PDF — e.g. three runs
for GTCO's FY2023, FY2024, FY2025 audited annual reports.

## Testing the pipeline without a real PDF

`tests/make_sample_pdf.py` generates a synthetic bank-report PDF (two
comparative year columns, figures in `N'000`, parenthesized negatives)
so you can sanity-check the extractor end to end:

```bash
python tests/make_sample_pdf.py
python extract.py --pdf tests/sample_bank_report.pdf --ticker GTCO \
  --period-end 2025-12-31 --source-doc "TEST" \
  --out tests/candidates_GTCO_2025.json
```

## Adding a new ticker or label variant

- New ticker: add it to `ENTITY_TYPE` in `config.py` (`"bank"` or
  `"nonbank"`).
- A label the extractor didn't recognize: add the regex variant to the
  relevant list in `LABEL_PATTERNS` in `config.py`.
